---
<PackLayout title="Make Claude use your skills: the pushy sentence and the test: Prompt &amp; Done">
  <main>
    <header>
      <p class="kicker"><a href="/">Prompt &amp; Done</a> · PUSHY pack</p>
      <h1>The one sentence that made Claude Haiku load the right skill 39 times out of 64 instead of 10.</h1>
      <p class="tag">Claude Code reads only each skill's name and <code>description</code> up front and decides from that whether to load the rest. A vague description is the usual reason a skill never fires.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">1</span> The pattern</h2>
      <pre><code>{sentence}</code></pre>
      <p>Put it at the end of the <code>description</code> in each skill's <code>SKILL.md</code>. This is the "make the skill descriptions a little bit pushy" advice from Anthropic's own <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-plugins-official/blob/main/plugins/skill-creator/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md">skill-creator</a>.</p>
      <pre><code>{example}</code></pre></section>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">2</span> What we measured (24 Oct 2026, Claude Code 2.1.309)</h2>
      <p>Eight skills (commit-message, sql-review, readme-writer, pr-description, bug-report, prompt-polish, changelog, test-writer) with identical bodies, in one project. 64 requests (8 per skill, written without naming the skill) and 16 unrelated questions per run. A request counts as a hit when the first replies load the right skill. Tools were limited to Skill, Read, Glob and Grep, 2 turns.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Haiku 5.5:</strong> plain description 10 / 64, with the pushy sentence 39 / 64 (one run each).</li>
        <li><strong>Sonnet 5.5:</strong> plain 98 / 128 (77%), pushy 121 / 128 (95%) (two runs each).</li>
        <li><strong>Unrelated questions:</strong> 0 of 160 loaded a skill, plain or pushy.</li>
        <li><strong>Opus 5.5:</strong> plain 116 / 128 (91%); we did not run Opus with the pushy sentence.</li>
        <li><strong>Per skill (Haiku, plain to pushy):</strong> commit-message 0 to 7 of 8, sql-review 1 to 5, readme-writer 2 to 6, pr-description 3 to 6, bug-report 4 to 8, prompt-polish 0 to 2, changelog 0 to 5, test-writer 0 to 0.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">Fair to say: this measures whether a skill loads, not whether the answer is better. The requests are ours, one project with eight skills, a restricted tool set. Bigger models already load skills well with a plain description. test-writer did not improve on Haiku.</p></section>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">3</span> Run it on your own skills</h2>
      <p>Save as <code>run_trigger_test.py</code> next to a <code>skills.json</code> shaped like the sample below (your skills' names, plain descriptions, a <code>triggers</code> phrase, an <code>also</code> phrase, a body and 8 indirect requests each; add unrelated questions under <code>negatives</code>). Run <code>python3 run_trigger_test.py claude-haiku-5-5 plain,pushy</code>. It needs Claude Code; every run costs real tokens (about 2 cents each on Haiku, more on larger models).</p>
      <pre><code>{harness}</code></pre>
      <p>skills.json (shape, two skills shown):</p>
      <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre></section>
  </main>
</PackLayout>
